  "slug": "image-diffractive-d927",
  "summary": "Preprints now apply stochastic optimisation, residual learning and wave-coupled networks to ptychography and diffractive optical design.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the coupling of learned or stochastic optimisation with explicit wave-optical physics in diffractive imaging and optics design. Stochastic gradient descent for X-ray ptychographic reconstruction reports peak GPU memory at 65% and time at 22% of a baseline, targeting computational scalability of phase retrieval. An EUV ptychography study applies residual learning trained on physics simulations to restore diffraction patterns, reporting an edge-response width of about 1.08 μm at 57 nm. On the design side, multiscale wave-coupled neural networks (MSWNets) combine fully connected networks with Rayleigh-Sommerfeld propagation in a PINN-inspired inverse-design formulation for broadband diffractive optics. A further preprint extends surrogate-guided design to incoherent multispectral diffractive optical networks for satellite image classification, a task distinct from reconstruction. What is new across these works is the embedding of propagation physics directly into the learning or optimisation loop.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
